Rule to remember:

One of the + superlative adjective + plural noun
one of the best writers ✅
one of the best singers ✅
one of the best players ✅
one of the best actors ✅

The Core Rule

When you have "one of the + plural noun + who/that + verb," 
the verb in the relative clause (who/that) agrees with the plural noun, not with "one" — because "who/that" is referring back to the group, not to the single person picked out.

But this flips when a word like "only" is added to mean "the sole one" — then it becomes singular, because now you're saying that person is uniquely different from the rest of the group.

Example 1: Mr. Sharma is one of our officers who are always alert.
"who" refers to "officers" (plural) — meaning: among all our alert officers, Sharma is one.
So the verb is plural → "are."
Meaning: many officers are alert; Sharma is one of them.


Example 2: Mr. Sharma is the only one of our officers who is always alert.
Here "the only one" changes everything — it singles Sharma out as the sole alert officer.
"who" now refers back to "the only one" (i.e., Sharma), not to the whole officer group.
So the verb is singular → "is."
Meaning: Sharma alone is alert; the rest are not.


Example 3: Mr. Sharma is one of the only officers who are always alert.
Tricky, but different from #2: "the only officers" is still a small plural group (a handful of officers, not one).
"who" refers to that plural group ("the only officers" = the few alert ones), so the verb stays plural → "are."
Meaning: Only a few officers are alert, and Sharma is one of that few.


Example 4: It is I who am responsible for the mistake.
"who" here refers back to "I," not to any plural noun.
Since "I" takes "am," the relative clause verb must also be "am," not "is."
(This trips people up because "It is..." makes them want to default to "is/who is.")

It is you who are responsible...(when the sub is you verb will be are not is)

Example 5: He is one of the best writers who/that have ever lived.
Same logic as Example 1: "who" refers to "writers" (plural) — the whole group of great writers throughout history.
So it's "have," not "has."
This is the exact same structure as "he is one of the best writers" we discussed earlier — the plural noun "writers" governs the verb.
